<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cimmerians</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th–7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cimmerians were a nomadic people from the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> who invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the eighth and seventh centuries BCE; Assyrian records call them Gimirri. They overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, killed the Lydian king Gyges in the mid-seventh century and raided the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, before being driven out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cimmerians.jpg" alt="Cimmerians" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antiquistik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cimmerian_Migrations.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the seventh century BCE the <b>Cimmerians</b> raided western Asia Minor, and later tradition said they threatened the Artemision.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From that century Scythians and <b>Cimmerians</b> migrating from Central Asia into the Pontic steppes invaded Colchis, and the Scythians made a base on the former territory of the kingdom from which they raided and campaigned in Media and Anatolia; Askold Ivanchik judges that Qulḫa, an independent flourishing state…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian records call him Gugu of Luddi and report that he sought the help of Ashurbanipal against the <b>Cimmerians</b>, nomadic invaders from beyond the Caucasus who had destroyed the Kingdom of Phrygia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Strabo and others, Midas killed himself by drinking bulls' blood when the <b>Cimmerians</b> overran his kingdom and sacked Gordium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 714 BCE Sargon II, informed by spies that Rusa I had just been defeated by the <b>Cimmerians</b>, marched round Lake Urmia, routed Rusa’s army in the mountains, devastated the country and sacked Musasir, carrying off the treasures of the temple of Haldi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The third account, which Herodotus himself preferred, held that the Scythians had been nomads in Asia who, pressed by the Massagetae, crossed the river Araxes and drove the <b>Cimmerians</b> from the steppe north of the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
